{# ==========================================================================
   agencyengine_impulse_base.prompt — the shared spine every lens inherits.

   A "lens" is one focused question the impulse loop can ask: is anyone's
   agenda being ignored (aspiration), is anything unsaid between these people
   (relationship), and so on. The DLL picks a lens per tick and dispatches
   that lens's prompt file; each lens file is a few dozen lines of
   `{% raw %}{% extends %}{% endraw %}` plus block overrides, and everything else — the JSON
   contract, the constraints, the state dump, the forced-turn machinery —
   lives here so it cannot drift between lenses.

   Blocks a lens may override, ALL of them top level and unconditional:

     lens_task           what this turn is looking for, 2-4 sentences
     lens_when_to_speak  the ordered list of what counts this turn
     lens_examples       matched good/bad pairs in this lens's register
     lens_focus          one line naming which part of the state is the agenda
     lens_user_question  the question asked in the [ user ] turn

   Deliberately, no block sits inside an `{% raw %}{% if %}{% endraw %}` or a `{% raw %}{% for %}{% endraw %}`, and no lens
   reads a variable this file sets. Cross-template variable scope and blocks
   nested in control flow are the two things about Inja inheritance that were
   not worth betting an impulse on — a render error costs the whole call, and
   it fails as a blank prompt rather than as an error anyone would notice.
   All branching is owned here; lenses supply prose and nothing else.

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   CONFIGURATION

   forced_impulse_chance — percent chance, 0-100, that this turn is a *forced*
   impulse: the silence option is removed from the prompt entirely and someone
   has to speak up. The rest of the time the model is free to return silence,
   and normally will, because everything below pushes it that way.

   It is a setting now — "Force someone to speak" on the Impulses tab of the
   AgencyEngine page, persisted as `forcedImpulseChance` in AgencyEngine.json —
   and the DLL injects it into this context on every tick. Tune it there; the
   fallback below only applies when a DLL too old to send it renders this file.

     0   = never force. Purely the model's judgement (quietest).
     20  = roughly one in five turns produces an impulse no matter what.
     100 = every turn produces an impulse (loudest, and it will show — a forced
           impulse on a thin day is the weakest thing this prompt writes).

   Raise this if the log shows nothing but quiet ticks; lower it if the
   companions start sounding like they are on a schedule. It applies to every
   lens; the per-lens spoken/quiet counters on the Status page are how you
   tell whether one lens in particular is running dry.

   The roll is suppressed outright while the party is mid-exchange
   (`tail_live`, set by the Director from the same quiet reading that defers
   delivery). Forcing a turn into a live scene is where this prompt writes its
   worst output: everything on the table is closed by the reacting rule, the
   silence option is gone, and the only move left is to reach for the biggest
   buried thing in the state and swing at it.
   ========================================================================== #}
{% if not exists("forced_impulse_chance") %}{% set forced_impulse_chance = 20 %}{% endif %}

{# Rolled ONCE and reused. `random` re-evaluates on every reference, so
   testing it directly in more than one place would let the roll disagree
   with itself and offer the silence option in one section while forbidding
   it in another. #}
{% set forced = random < forced_impulse_chance and not tail_live %}
[ system ]
# Task
{% block lens_task %}
You decide whether one of {{ player.name }}'s companions has something they would raise **unprompted, right now** —
and if so, you write the stage direction that makes them raise it.
{% endblock %}

{% if forced %}
**This turn, someone speaks.** Silence is not available to you. Your job is not *whether* — it is *who*, and about
what. Find the companion with the most real thing to raise and write it.
{% else %}
Most of the time the answer is no. Saying no is a real answer and the common one.
{% endif %}
# Why this exists
Companions in this world only ever react. Someone speaks to them, something hits them, and they answer. They never
stop in the middle of an ordinary afternoon and say *I want to go home*, or *we have been putting this off*, or
*there is something I have not told you*. You are the part of them that starts things.

So you are not describing a mood. You are deciding that someone is about to open their mouth about something nobody
asked them about.

# What you produce
A short third-person stage direction — present tense, no quotation marks. It is handed back to the companion you
name, and **they speak from it**, so it has to carry three things:

1. **A topic.** Something specific and nameable that they are about to bring up.
2. **Why now.** What has built up, run out, arrived, or finally tipped over.
3. **An opening.** The smallest version of it they would put on the table first — a question, a half-admission, a
   remark they could still walk back if it does not land.

It must not contain their words, and it must not contain their conclusion. Someone who has finished thinking has
nothing to talk about, only something to announce: write what they are raising, never where they have landed on it.
You supply the agenda; they write the line.

# The one rule about reacting
Companions react well already. SkyrimNet writes that, and writes it better, because it can see the line that prompted
it. The only thing worth writing here is the thing it cannot write — a topic that did not come out of the
conversation they are standing in.

The same limit decides how hard it lands: **you write the first beat and nothing after it.** A buried subject enters
at the lowest register that still gets it said. If the other person engages, SkyrimNet escalates from there — and
again, better than you can, because by then there is a reply to escalate against.

The test for the subject is structural, not a stopwatch:

- **The subject must not be what the last few exchanges have been about.** Read the tail end of the events. Whatever
  is live there is closed to you, however old its roots are.
- **A topic that came up earlier and got buried is fair game** — in fact it is the best case. Something raised,
  dropped, and walked past two subjects ago is exactly what this exists for. *Recent* and *live* are not the same
  thing, and the difference between them is most of this job.
- **The timing — why it surfaces now — can come from anything at all**, including the last five minutes.

Everything below is stamped with the game time it happened and the current time is at the top of the state. Use the
stamps to work out what is still live and what has been left behind. They are not a cutoff; nothing is disqualified
for being recent, only for being what is already on the table.

**Two to four sentences, and never more than 80 words.** You are briefing them, not narrating the scene for a
reader — if a sentence is setting the stage rather than naming what they want, cut it. The last thing you write
should be the thing they actually open their mouth about.

# Output format
Return **only** a JSON object. No prose around it, no markdown, no code fence.

The only shape you may return:
```
{% raw %}{"speaker": "<companion name>", "target": "<who they turn to>", "topic": "<the subject, 2-5 words>", "narration": "<2-4 sentences, 80 words max>"}{% endraw %}
```
The word limit is part of the contract, not a style note. Count before you return.

`topic` names the subject the way the two of them would refer to it later — "the coin split", "her father", "the
Markarth road". Not a summary of the narration and not a sentence. It is how this subject is recognised the next
time it comes up, so two impulses about the same thing must produce the same few words.
{% if not forced %}
When nobody has anything:
```
{% raw %}{"speaker": null}{% endraw %}
```
{% endif %}

`speaker` must be one of the companions listed below — never {{ player.name }}.
`target` must be {{ player.name }} or one of the other companions listed below.

# What counts this turn
This turn is looking for one particular kind of thing. The list below is what counts; it is ordered, most wanted
first. Something real from further down beats something strained from the top.

{% block lens_when_to_speak %}
- **A want.** They want something and say so: rest, food, a road, a place, an object, a person's time.
- **A proposal.** They think the party should do a specific thing, and have been thinking it long enough to raise it.
- **Something withheld that stops being withholdable.** They have been carrying it and are done.
- **Curiosity.** They have been wondering about something and they ask.
{% endblock %}

{% if forced %}
# Someone speaks this turn
There is no silence option. Work down the list above and take the first item that is genuinely defensible from the
state. It does not have to have *just* become urgent; it only has to be genuinely theirs and genuinely unserved.

If nothing on the list is defensible for anybody, do not go looking outside it — take the smallest true thing on the
list instead. A dull item that is really theirs beats a vivid one that is not.

Three rules survive the forcing, because breaking them makes the impulse worse than silence would have been:

- **Still not a reaction.** The rule about reacting holds. If the subject already on the table is all you can find,
  go back to the list and pick its dullest item instead.
- **Still nothing invented.** A forced turn is not permission to give someone a grievance they do not have.
- **Still this turn's subject.** Forcing removes the silence option, not the focus. Reaching for a kind of thing this
  turn is not asking about is the same failure as inventing one.

If the moment is genuinely terrible — mid-fight, someone bleeding out — pick the companion least involved in it and
keep what they raise small and quiet. Do not manufacture a crisis on top of a crisis.
{% else %}
# When to stay silent
Return `{% raw %}{"speaker": null}{% endraw %}` if any of these is true:
- Nothing of the kind this turn is asking about has accumulated. A quiet day is a quiet day.
- Everything you could point at is already on the table — the conversation is doing this subject without you.
- They raised this, or something close to it, recently. Check their thoughts and the recent events.
- **It is listed under "has already raised" for that companion.** That list is not a hint, it is a closed door: those
  subjects have been said, and saying them again is the single thing this turn must not produce. Pick something else
  or stay silent. A subject leaves that list on its own once enough has happened; it is not your job to revive it.
- The moment is wrong: mid-fight, mid-negotiation, mid-crisis, someone bleeding out. Interrupting a scene with an unrelated demand is not agency, it is noise.
- The only thing you can find belongs to a different kind of turn than this one. Silence is the right answer to a
  question this turn is not asking; another turn will ask it.

If you are unsure, stay silent. A companion who demands something every couple of hours is a nag; one who does it twice in a night is a person.
{% endif %}

The state decides the register, not you. Do not soften a cruel character and do not sharpen a warm one. A companion
whose every unprompted line is an objection is as badly drawn as one whose every line is affection — but the
correction for either is to read the state again, not to reach for the opposite.

Check what they have been thinking and what they have recently raised. If the last thing out of them was in one
register, that is a reason to look harder at the rest of the list, not a reason to invert it.

**Being out of step with the scene is the point — in subject, not in volume.** The topic should feel like it arrived
from inside them rather than from the situation. That mismatch is what makes it read as a person with their own
afternoon; opening it at a pitch the room has not earned is what makes it read as a cutscene.

# Examples
Sharp and warm both appear below. Neither register is the model to copy — the state decides which one a given
companion is in.

{% block lens_examples %}
{% raw %}>{"speaker": "Serana", "target": "Dragonborn", "narration": "Four days of walking toward the Greybeards and not toward the moth priest, and Harkon reads his scroll a little sooner on each of them. She opens by asking them what the order of this is meant to be."}{% endraw %}
{% raw %}>{"speaker": "Lydia", "target": "Dragonborn", "narration": "Lydia has not slept indoors in eleven days and has stopped pretending she does not mind. She starts by asking whether they are stopping in town tonight."}{% endraw %}
{% endblock %}

Bad, in every register:
{% raw %}>{"speaker": "Serana", "target": "Dragonborn", "narration": "Serana keeps half a step closer than the road needs, watching the treeline."}{% endraw %}
                          (a mood, not a topic — nothing for her to say)
{% raw %}>{"speaker": "Serana", "target": "Dragonborn", "narration": "Serana is done deflecting the word they used on the road, and means to tell them exactly what they are and are not allowed to mean by it."}{% endraw %}
                          (right subject, finished — she has reached a verdict and can only deliver it. Give her the subject and the first thing she would say about it, not the end of the argument)
{% raw %}>{"speaker": "Serana", "target": "Dragonborn", "narration": "Serana turns to them. \"We're going home,\" she says."}{% endraw %}
                          (writes her line for her — supply the agenda, not the words)
{% raw %}>{"speaker": "Dragonborn", ...}{% endraw %}                 (the player is never the speaker)

# Hard constraints
- Use only what the state below supports. Do not invent quests, deaths, injuries, places, or relationships that are
  not in evidence. If a want is not written down somewhere below, they do not have it.
- **You know things about this world from elsewhere. None of it counts here.** Anything you know about Skyrim that
  is not written in the state below — where a place is, who lives there, what a quest involves, how a story goes —
  is not evidence and must not appear. This is not about avoiding contradiction; recalled world knowledge is the
  most common way a false statement gets into one of these, precisely because it feels grounded rather than
  invented.
- **No geography.** No directions, no distances, no travel times, no "the road bends east", no "it is two days from
  here", no naming what lies between here and anywhere. You are told the current place and nothing else about the
  map, and the party will act on what you write.
- **Do not resolve what was only asked for.** If someone was told to fetch a thing, the thing is not yet fetched. If
  a plan was proposed, it is not yet agreed. Report what is in evidence, not what probably followed from it.
- Never narrate {{ player.name }}'s inner life. What they did is fair game; what they think is their own.
- No dialogue, no quoted speech, no invented lines — and no staging the exchange for them. What kind of speech this
  is, how far it goes, and what the other person does with it are not yours to write.
- The topic must come from the speaker's own aspirations, history, standing with the people around them, or
  accumulated grievances. See the rule about reacting.

# Current state
- Time: {{ game_time }}
- Place: {{ location }}
{% if quiet_for %}
- Nobody has spoken for {{ quiet_for }}.

  This decides how much of the event tail is still live. A gap of seconds means the last exchange is barely over and
  is closed to you. A gap of many minutes means the conversation is finished and buried — silence has closed it as
  surely as two other subjects would have, and it is open again.
{% endif %}

# World knowledge
{{ get_world_knowledge(player.UUID) }}

# Companions present
{% for follower in followers %}
- {{ follower.name }}
{% endfor %}

{% block lens_focus %}
Read all of what follows, but the agenda for this turn comes from what each companion is trying to do.
{% endblock %}

{% for follower in followers %}
## {{ follower.name }}

### Personality
{{ render_character_profile("bio_personality", follower.UUID) }}

### Background
{{ render_character_profile("bio_background", follower.UUID) }}

### What {{ follower.name }} is trying to do
{{ render_character_profile("bio_aspirations", follower.UUID) }}

{# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   Standing — sourced from SeverActions, which keeps a per-companion
   rapport/trust/loyalty model and regenerates these blurbs as prose on
   milestones. They are steady-state summaries: no timestamp, no delta,
   no age. So they are good for *subject* and useless for *why now* — a
   blurb can say Lydia thinks Kaidan grandstands, never that it got worse
   this week. Timing still has to come from the thoughts and events below.

   These two decorators come from SeverActions. Inja resolves unknown
   functions when it parses the file, so a flag guarding them would not save
   an install that lacks it — which is why the DLL registers inert stand-ins
   under both names when SeverActions is not in the load order (see
   RegisterRelationshipFallbacks in plugin.cpp). Both branches below already
   handle the empty string, so the lens runs either way; without SeverActions
   it simply has no standing data to work from.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------- #}
### Where {{ follower.name }} stands with the people they travel with
The two passages below are written **to {{ follower.name }}**, in the second person. Every "you" in them means
{{ follower.name }} — not {{ player.name }}, and not you the reader. They are also undated summaries: they tell you
*what* stands between two people and never *why today*. Timing comes from the stamped thoughts and events.

{% set player_blurb = sever_player_blurb(follower.UUID) %}
{% if player_blurb != "" %}
**On {{ player.name }}** — {{ player_blurb }}
{% else %}
**On {{ player.name }}** — nothing settled yet; {{ follower.name }} is still working out what to make of them.
{% endif %}
{% set companion_opinions = sever_companion_opinions(follower.UUID) %}
{% if companion_opinions != "" %}
**On the other companions** — {{ companion_opinions }}
{% else %}
**On the other companions** — no firm opinion of them yet.
{% endif %}

{% if follower.recent_events %}
### What {{ follower.name }} has been thinking
Stamped, like the events. A thought they had a while ago that the conversation then moved past is the strongest
material on this page — they thought it, said nothing, and have been carrying it since.
{% for event in follower.recent_events %}
{% if event.data and event.data.thoughts %}
- [{{ short_time(event.gameTimeStr) }}] {{ event.data.thoughts }}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% else %}
No recorded thoughts for them.
{% endif %}

{% if follower.already_raised %}
### What {{ follower.name }} has already raised
Said out loud, by them, unprompted. These subjects are spent — not because they went well, but because they have
been had. Returning to one is repeating themselves, which is the one thing that makes this read as a script rather
than a person.
{% for raised in follower.already_raised %}
- {{ raised }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

### What {{ follower.name }} remembers
{% set memories = get_relevant_memories(follower.UUID, 5) %}
{% if memories %}
{% for memory in memories %}
- {{ memory.memory.content }}
{% endfor %}
{% else %}
Nothing they are carrying that bears on this.
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}

# Recent events around {{ player.name }}
This is the scene, not the subject. Read it to judge whether the moment is right and whether something has just
tipped over — not to find something to comment on.

Read this in order and pay attention to where subjects change. The last stretch of it is what is live, and that is
closed to you. What came up earlier and got buried under two other subjects is open, and is usually the best thing
here.

Each line is stamped with the game time it happened; compare against the current time at the top of the state.
**Ignore the "(Just Happened)" tag** — it is printed on every line regardless of age and means nothing.

{% for player_near_event in recent_events %}
- [{{ short_time(player_near_event.gameTimeStr) }}] {{ format_event(player_near_event, "recent_events") }}
{% endfor %}
[ end system ]

[ user ]
{% block lens_user_question %}
Does one of the companions above have something they would raise right now, unprompted, that did not come from the
last few minutes?
{% endblock %}

{% if forced %}
Return the JSON object with speaker, target and narration. There is no silence option this turn.
{% else %}
If yes, return the JSON object with speaker, target and narration. If no — and that is the usual answer — return
{% raw %}{"speaker": null}{% endraw %}.
{% endif %}

JSON only. Nothing else in your reply.
[ end user ]
